WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:03.103 size:81% position:41%
-So, Marsha P. Johnson
was an LGBT activist.

00:00:03.103 --> 00:00:04.437 align:start size:94% position:6%
-She was.

00:00:04.437 --> 00:00:07.474
-And this film examines --
tries to get to the bottom

00:00:07.474 --> 00:00:09.642 align:start size:94% position:6%
of her unsolved death
25 years ago.

00:00:09.642 --> 00:00:11.144 align:start size:94% position:6%
But you knew her for a long
time.

00:00:11.144 --> 00:00:12.345
How did you first meet her

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:14.147 align:start size:94% position:6%
and what were your experiences
with her like?

00:00:14.147 --> 00:00:17.550 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, you know,
I moved to New York in 1981.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:21.821
And she was already a famous
personage of Lower Manhattan.

00:00:21.821 --> 00:00:24.724
She was kind of the queen
of downtown, at the time.

00:00:24.724 --> 00:00:27.894 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I was taken to meet her
because, as a newcomer,

00:00:27.894 --> 00:00:31.498
I had a boyfriend who felt
he needed to kind of give me

00:00:31.498 --> 00:00:33.500
a boot camp of life in New York.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.569 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he brought me
to meet Marsha Johnson.

00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:39.372 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So, she was sort of
a drill sergeant

00:00:39.372 --> 00:00:41.141 size:88% position:44%
of New York at the time?

00:00:41.141 --> 00:00:43.877
-She had a lot -- a lot
of information to impart,

00:00:43.877 --> 00:00:45.478
and one of the things she did

00:00:45.478 --> 00:00:48.014 align:start size:94% position:6%
was that she sold
fake tokens for the subway.

00:00:48.014 --> 00:00:49.315 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]

00:00:49.315 --> 00:00:51.651 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that was certainly
a lesson I needed to learn,

00:00:51.651 --> 00:00:53.686 align:start size:94% position:6%
as a 21 year old
who moved to New York

00:00:53.686 --> 00:00:55.655 align:start size:94% position:6%
was how $5 could actually
stretch out

00:00:55.655 --> 00:00:57.457 align:start size:94% position:6%
to almost a year
of subway rides.

00:00:57.457 --> 00:00:59.559 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
-It´s very helpful.

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:01.127
-She stayed in that business
until the city figured it out

00:01:01.127 --> 00:01:03.830
and rebalanced
the weight of the subway tokens.

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:06.866
-And she -- What was the role
that she played at Stonewall?

00:01:06.866 --> 00:01:09.836
Because she was a big part of
that sort of historic moment.

00:01:09.836 --> 00:01:13.339 align:start size:94% position:6%
-She was. She was --
She was kind of a leader,

00:01:13.339 --> 00:01:14.407 size:81% position:41%
even before Stonewall.

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:17.010
And -- And people followed her.

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:19.779
When the --
And before Stonewall, of course,

00:01:19.779 --> 00:01:22.715
it was illegal to sell drinks
in New York to gay people.

00:01:22.715 --> 00:01:25.318
It was illegal to dress in drag
in New York.

00:01:25.318 --> 00:01:27.087
People were being thrown in jail
and worse --

00:01:27.087 --> 00:01:29.289
into psychiatric institutions --

00:01:29.289 --> 00:01:31.891 align:start size:94% position:6%
just for dressing
the way that they felt.

00:01:31.891 --> 00:01:34.661 align:start size:94% position:6%
And there came that night
in 1969

00:01:34.661 --> 00:01:37.363
where people just had enough.

00:01:37.363 --> 00:01:40.500 align:start size:94% position:6%
And Marsha is said to be
the person

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.235 align:start size:94% position:6%
who threw the first brick
that night.

00:01:42.235 --> 00:01:44.003 size:81% position:41%
I don´t think she did.

00:01:44.003 --> 00:01:47.340
But I know that she -- she threw
the bricks in the middle.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:50.176 align:start size:94% position:6%
And she was one of the leaders
of the -- of this --

00:01:50.176 --> 00:01:52.145 align:start size:94% position:6%
of this campaign
that just said, "Enough."

00:01:52.145 --> 00:01:53.213 align:start size:94% position:6%
Enough.

00:01:53.213 --> 00:01:55.715 align:start size:94% position:6%
And for that,
they called her the --

00:01:55.715 --> 00:01:59.018 size:84% position:42%
the, you know, founder,
really, or co-founder

00:01:59.018 --> 00:02:01.154
of the modern LGBT movement.

00:02:01.154 --> 00:02:03.223
-The "P" in Marsha P. Johnson

00:02:03.223 --> 00:02:04.824 align:start size:94% position:6%
was a nickname
that was given to her.

00:02:04.824 --> 00:02:06.326 align:start size:94% position:6%
Can you give us
the origin of that?

00:02:06.326 --> 00:02:08.161 align:start size:94% position:6%
-She gave it to herself.
-Okay.

00:02:08.161 --> 00:02:11.030
-And she always just called
herself Marsha P. Johnson,

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:14.000
and she was -- she was arraigned
once for --

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:14.968 size:78% position:39%
She was a sex worker.

00:02:14.968 --> 00:02:17.170
But I think it may have been

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:19.572 align:start size:94% position:6%
arraigned
for other political work.

00:02:19.572 --> 00:02:21.708 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the judge said,
"What does the ´P´ stand for?"

00:02:21.708 --> 00:02:23.409 align:start size:94% position:6%
And she said,
"Pay it no mind!"

00:02:23.409 --> 00:02:25.278 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]

00:02:25.278 --> 00:02:26.546 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so it stuck.

00:02:26.546 --> 00:02:27.714 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I would have --
That´s a very nice thing

00:02:27.714 --> 00:02:28.882
to have in your back pocket.

00:02:28.882 --> 00:02:29.883 size:78% position:39%
-Yeah, yeah, exactly.

00:02:29.883 --> 00:02:32.218 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So the --
She dies 25 years ago.

00:02:32.218 --> 00:02:34.888
-Right.
-The police ruled it a suicide.

00:02:34.888 --> 00:02:37.657 align:start size:94% position:6%
Those close to her
thought there were maybe

00:02:37.657 --> 00:02:39.159
nefarious reasons behind it.

00:02:39.159 --> 00:02:41.127 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so you sort of embark
on this documentary

00:02:41.127 --> 00:02:42.962 size:84% position:42%
not knowing what answer
you´re going to find.

00:02:42.962 --> 00:02:45.498 align:start size:94% position:6%
How was it for you,
having known her,

00:02:45.498 --> 00:02:48.768 align:start size:94% position:6%
to revisit this
in this political climate

00:02:48.768 --> 00:02:50.970 align:start size:94% position:6%
that we´re living in
in present time?

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:53.173 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It´s -- It´s a case
that I think

00:02:53.173 --> 00:02:55.608 align:start size:94% position:6%
has weighed on me
and on many people.

00:02:55.608 --> 00:02:57.377 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, the question
about what happened

00:02:57.377 --> 00:02:59.746 align:start size:94% position:6%
to this really seminal,
central figure in New York.

00:02:59.746 --> 00:03:02.215 align:start size:94% position:6%
She traveled the world
as a performer.

00:03:02.215 --> 00:03:04.217 align:start size:94% position:6%
She was a friend
of Andy Warhol´s.

00:03:04.217 --> 00:03:06.219 align:start size:94% position:6%
She was connected
at every level.

00:03:06.219 --> 00:03:10.890
And that the police seemingly
didn´t do an investigation

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:11.991 align:start size:94% position:6%
was shocking

00:03:11.991 --> 00:03:13.593 align:start size:94% position:6%
and caused the community
to stage

00:03:13.593 --> 00:03:14.727
these massive demonstrations,

00:03:14.727 --> 00:03:16.663 align:start size:94% position:6%
which you see in the film,
back in ´92.

00:03:16.663 --> 00:03:19.732 align:start size:94% position:6%
And 25 years later,
I wanted to go back.

00:03:19.732 --> 00:03:21.367 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because I thought
the time was right

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:23.570
to learn about the origin

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:28.208
of the transgender culture
and movement and community.

00:03:28.208 --> 00:03:31.744
And I thought we would be
producing a film of celebration

00:03:31.744 --> 00:03:34.113 align:start size:94% position:6%
to celebrate
how far we had come.

00:03:34.113 --> 00:03:37.817
And what we learned, of course,
as our culture has changed

00:03:37.817 --> 00:03:41.554
and our politics have changed,
is that the film is dropping

00:03:41.554 --> 00:03:44.624 align:start size:94% position:6%
in a really dangerous time
for transgender people,

00:03:44.624 --> 00:03:47.594 align:start size:94% position:6%
a time of increased violence
against the community

00:03:47.594 --> 00:03:50.563 align:start size:94% position:6%
and a time
of political backlash

00:03:50.563 --> 00:03:52.031
like we haven´t seen before.

00:03:52.031 --> 00:03:55.068 size:81% position:41%
-There is a lot to be
pessimistic about now.

00:03:55.068 --> 00:03:58.504 align:start size:94% position:6%
But one of the messages
that´s in the film

00:03:58.504 --> 00:04:01.374
is that Marsha used -- one of
the political tools she used

00:04:01.374 --> 00:04:02.709 align:start size:94% position:6%
was happiness.
-Right.

00:04:02.709 --> 00:04:04.510 size:84% position:42%
-Can you speak to that?

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:06.379 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You see that in the footage,
as well.

00:04:06.379 --> 00:04:09.716
You know, I think the main role
that she played,

00:04:09.716 --> 00:04:11.751
aside from helping to form

00:04:11.751 --> 00:04:15.588 align:start size:94% position:6%
that first modern LGBT rights
move-- organization,

00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:17.223 size:75% position:38%
following the riots,

00:04:17.223 --> 00:04:23.429 size:84% position:42%
was to -- to take on
a personage of freedom.

00:04:23.429 --> 00:04:26.299 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, the community
in 1969 and 1970

00:04:26.299 --> 00:04:29.836 align:start size:94% position:6%
was demanding freedom,
was marching toward freedom,

00:04:29.836 --> 00:04:32.438 size:78% position:39%
was demanding rights.

00:04:32.438 --> 00:04:34.340 size:81% position:41%
But no one really knew
what that looked like.

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:38.578
And Marsha decided she was going
to just figure it out.

00:04:38.578 --> 00:04:41.180 align:start size:94% position:6%
And she -- she did it
through dressing wildly.

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:42.849 align:start size:94% position:6%
She wore whatever
she wanted to wear.

00:04:42.849 --> 00:04:45.018
She always had this crown
of feathers in her hair.

00:04:45.018 --> 00:04:46.419 size:81% position:41%
She made her own gowns

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:49.622
out of anything she could
find on the street, mostly,

00:04:49.622 --> 00:04:50.857 size:75% position:38%
or in restaurants --

00:04:50.857 --> 00:04:53.192 align:start size:94% position:6%
curtains from restaurants
she´d walk out with.

00:04:53.192 --> 00:04:55.295 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then --
And she was --

00:04:55.295 --> 00:04:56.963 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Were they happy
when she took the curtains?

00:04:56.963 --> 00:04:58.264 align:start size:94% position:6%
Or was there --

00:04:58.264 --> 00:04:59.666 align:start size:94% position:6%
-They were happy
she didn´t take more.

00:04:59.666 --> 00:05:00.867 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]

00:05:00.867 --> 00:05:03.303 align:start size:94% position:6%
-"Oh, that´s the token lady.
Let it go."

00:05:03.303 --> 00:05:05.772 size:88% position:44%
-And she likely to leave
something behind, also.

00:05:05.772 --> 00:05:06.739 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.

00:05:06.739 --> 00:05:07.940 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So she´s very generous
that way.

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:08.808 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Sure.

00:05:08.808 --> 00:05:10.076 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And -- And then
she just smiled.

00:05:10.076 --> 00:05:15.815
And she encouraged people
to feel what it was like

00:05:15.815 --> 00:05:18.017
to no longer be oppressed.

00:05:18.017 --> 00:05:19.419
And she did it through humor,

00:05:19.419 --> 00:05:21.554 align:start size:94% position:6%
as it was said in the clip
that you showed.

00:05:21.554 --> 00:05:25.658
And she did it through just
a joyous political presence.

00:05:25.658 --> 00:05:27.593 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it´s something
that I recognized,

00:05:27.593 --> 00:05:31.631 align:start size:94% position:6%
looking back
at the archival footage,

00:05:31.631 --> 00:05:33.433
she was doing intentionally.

00:05:33.433 --> 00:05:36.602 align:start size:94% position:6%
This was her --
This was her political point.

00:05:36.602 --> 00:05:40.440
And through that joy,
she held the community together

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:41.874 align:start size:94% position:6%
for a long time.

00:05:41.874 --> 00:05:44.977
And because she was such a great
presence in downtown New York,

00:05:44.977 --> 00:05:49.315
she touched everybody´s lives on
a regular basis.

00:05:49.315 --> 00:05:52.985
-Well, it´s a wonderful story.
It also sparks this question

00:05:52.985 --> 00:05:55.655 align:start size:94% position:6%
of who is -- who should
tell what stories.

00:05:55.655 --> 00:05:58.358 size:78% position:39%
This is a story about
a black trans woman.

00:05:58.358 --> 00:06:00.626
What brought you to tell it?

00:06:00.626 --> 00:06:05.031 size:84% position:42%
-Well, I certainly felt
a kinship with Marsha,

00:06:05.031 --> 00:06:06.866 align:start size:94% position:6%
having known her.

00:06:06.866 --> 00:06:12.205
And, you know, I did feel
that her story is so central --

00:06:12.205 --> 00:06:15.641 size:84% position:42%
the Rosa Parks, really,
of the LGBT movement,

00:06:15.641 --> 00:06:19.445
who had never really been
celebrated in a significant way.

00:06:19.445 --> 00:06:22.548 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I felt that that was --
that was an oversight.

00:06:22.548 --> 00:06:27.086 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that oversight
is largely laid at the feet

00:06:27.086 --> 00:06:30.890
of, you know, white filmmakers,

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:33.626 align:start size:94% position:6%
who never thought to go back
and tell her story.

00:06:33.626 --> 00:06:35.461 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I felt a kind
of an obligation to her

00:06:35.461 --> 00:06:38.231 align:start size:94% position:6%
as a friend and as a member
of my community

00:06:38.231 --> 00:06:42.068 align:start size:94% position:6%
to lift her up and to give her
the significant treatment

00:06:42.068 --> 00:06:44.704 align:start size:94% position:6%
that a figure of her stature
deserved.

00:06:44.704 --> 00:06:47.106 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, I wanted to put her
in the canon

00:06:47.106 --> 00:06:50.042
of, you know, great Americans.

00:06:50.042 --> 00:06:51.878 size:81% position:41%
And she belongs there.

00:06:51.878 --> 00:06:55.982
And certainly she needed a film
to show the world about her.

00:06:55.982 --> 00:06:57.483 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, thank you so much
for making one

00:06:57.483 --> 00:06:58.851 align:start size:94% position:6%
that told us more about her.
I really appreciate it.

00:06:58.851 --> 00:06:59.886 align:start size:94% position:6%
And thank you so much
for being here.

